{"name":"Zircon","hex":"#dee3e3","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"zircon","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A mineral occurring in tetragonal crystals, usually of a brown or grey colour and consisting of silica and zirconia.","A crystal of zircon, sometimes used as a false or semiprecious gemstone."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/zircon"}],"entries":[{"title":"Zircon","blocks":[{"text":"Zircon is a mineral belonging to the group of nesosilicates and is a source of the metal zirconium. Its chemical name is zirconium(IV) silicate, and its corresponding chemical formula is ZrSiO4. An empirical formula showing some of the range of substitution in zircon is (Zr1–y, REEy)(SiO4)1–x(OH)4x–y. Zircon precipitates from silicate melts and has relatively high concentrations of high field strength incompatible elements. For example, hafnium is almost always present in quantities ranging from 1 to 4%. The crystal structure of zircon is tetragonal crystal system. The natural color of zircon varies between colorless, yellow-golden, red, brown, blue, and green."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Zircon-dtn1a.jpg/330px-Zircon-dtn1a.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zircon"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/Zircon_microscope.jpg/330px-Zircon_microscope.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/Zircon_microscope.jpg/330px-Zircon_microscope.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Optical microscope photograph; the length of the crystal is about 250 μm","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AZircon_microscope.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9c/ZirconUSGOV.jpg/500px-ZirconUSGOV.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9c/ZirconUSGOV.jpg/500px-ZirconUSGOV.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Sand-sized grains of zircon","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AZirconUSGOV.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c8/ZirkonBlau.jpg/330px-ZirkonBlau.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c8/ZirkonBlau.jpg/330px-ZirkonBlau.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A pale blue zircon gemstone weighing 3.36 carats","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AZirkonBlau.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/Zircon_Bracelet.jpg/500px-Zircon_Bracelet.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/Zircon_Bracelet.jpg/1280px-Zircon_Bracelet.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/Zircon_Bracelet.jpg/500px-Zircon_Bracelet.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"This bracelet has zircon gemstones. The metal is zinc alloy base with silver coating.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AZircon_Bracelet.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f9/Zircon_grain_%28CL-SEM_imaging%29.tiff/lossy-page1-500px-Zircon_grain_%28CL-SEM_imaging%29.tiff.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f9/Zircon_grain_%28CL-SEM_imaging%29.tiff/lossy-page1-500px-Zircon_grain_%28CL-SEM_imaging%29.tiff.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"SEM-CL image